sonix.demuxer/--background--

 DESCRIPTION
   A Reggae demultiplexer for the framed byte stream sonix.stream produces.
   It strips the framing described in sonixwire.h, one SnxStreamHeader
   followed by any number of SnxFrameHeader plus picture pairs, and hands
   the pictures on as MMF_VIDEO_RGB24 or MMF_VIDEO_GRAY8.

   Input port 0 carries MMF_STREAM and is the sonix.stream object in front
   of it, output port 1 carries the video. The geometry and the pixel format
   are not configured by the application: they arrive in the stream header
   and are published on port 1 as soon as they have been read, which happens
   on the first MMM_Pull on port 1 or on an MMM_Setup on port 0, whichever
   comes first. An application which wants to size its buffers beforehand
   therefore has to set the pipeline up before it can ask for the width.

   The class is a demuxer for a live source, so it never reports
   MMERR_END_OF_DATA: the stream only ends when the camera is unplugged or
   stops answering, which comes back as MMERR_IO_ERROR. It is not seekable,
   and MMA_Video_FrameCount is 0 because a live stream has no count.

   A stream which is not in step cannot be resynchronised. A source behind
   it which is not a sonix.stream, a header which does not describe a Sonix
   stream, a frame size which does not match the header, and a source which
   stops in the middle of a header or a frame are all refused with
   MMERR_WRONG_DATA or MMERR_IO_ERROR, and the class then stays refused:
   every later pull returns 0 and reports the error of the first failure.
   Reading on after that would take whatever comes next for a frame header,
   so the object does not pretend a picture is there.

   A source which has not been set up yet is a different thing and is not a
   failure: nothing comes back from it, the pull is refused without an error
   of its own, and the next one reads the header. The same is true of a
   frame buffer which could not be allocated, because the stream is still in
   step there and a later pull may get the frame out of it.

   The frame rate is measured rather than taken from the stream header,
   which is a hint at best and is zero on a source which has just been
   opened. It is derived from the fh_Timestamp of the frames, and nothing is
   known until two of them have been read, which is reported as a rate of 0
   rather than as a made up one.

 NEW ATTRIBUTES
   Attributes applicability:
     I - may be set at creation time.
     S - may be set on an existing object.
     G - may be get from an object.
     P - may be set for an object's port.
     Q - may be queried from an object's port.

   MMA_Sonix_VendorID   (V1)     [..G], UWORD
   MMA_Sonix_ProductID  (V1)     [..G], UWORD
   MMA_Sonix_Sensor     (V1)     [..G], ULONG
   MMA_Sonix_FrameIndex (V1)     [..G], ULONG
   MMA_Sonix_FrameTime  (V1)     [..G], ULONG

 NEW METHODS
   MMM_Pull(port, buffer, length)  (V1)
sonix.demuxer/MMA_Sonix_FrameIndex

 NAME
   MMA_Sonix_FrameIndex (V1) [..G], ULONG

 FUNCTION
   Index of the last frame which has been read off the input, taken from
   fh_Index of its frame header, and 0 until the first one has been. The
   source counts from 0 every time it is set up, so this counts frames
   within one run and is not a total over the lifetime of the camera.

 NOTES
   This is the index of the frame, not the number of frames which have been
   served. The sonix.stream object in front counts those itself and reports
   them as its own MMA_StreamPosFrames.

sonix.demuxer/MMA_Sonix_FrameTime

 NAME
   MMA_Sonix_FrameTime (V1) [..G], ULONG

 FUNCTION
   Measured time between two frames in microseconds, and 0 until two frames
   have been read. It is a running average over the frames read so far, so a
   camera which slips a frame does not move it much. A timestamp which went
   backwards or jumped by more than two seconds is not a frame time and is
   left out of the average.

 NOTES
   Together with MMA_Video_FpsNumerator and MMA_Video_FpsDenominator this is
   the rate the camera is really running at. The nominal frame time in the
   stream header is not used: it is a hint, and it is zero on a source which
   has only just been opened.

sonix.demuxer/MMA_Sonix_ProductID

 NAME
   MMA_Sonix_ProductID (V1) [..G], UWORD

 FUNCTION
   USB product id of the camera which was claimed, taken from the stream
   header, and 0 before the header has been read. It is read only, it has no
   meaning as a selector here: the camera is picked by the sonix.stream
   object in front of this one.

 NOTES
   The same number is set on the sonix.stream object as a selector, where it
   defaults to 0 and matches a camera of any product. Which camera answered
   is only known once a stream header has been read.

sonix.demuxer/MMA_Sonix_Sensor

 NAME
   MMA_Sonix_Sensor (V1) [..G], ULONG

 FUNCTION
   Sensor found behind the sn9c102 controller of the camera which was
   claimed, one of the SNXS_* values of sonixwire.h: SNXS_PAS106B,
   SNXS_TAS5110C1B or SNXS_PAC207. It is taken from the stream header, so it
   is SNXS_NONE until the header has been read. The sensor is probed over
   I2C by the sonix.stream object, which refuses a camera with neither a
   PAS106B nor a TAS5110C1B on MMM_Setup with MMERR_NO_STREAM_CLASS.

 NOTES
   The name of the sensor is not republished, it is a string and would have
   to be translated. sonix.stream answers MMA_Sonix_SensorName with a plain
   ASCII name, which is readable on the object in front.

sonix.demuxer/MMA_Sonix_VendorID

 NAME
   MMA_Sonix_VendorID (V1) [..G], UWORD

 FUNCTION
   USB vendor id of the camera which was claimed, taken from the stream
   header, and 0 before the header has been read. It is read only, it has no
   meaning as a selector here: the camera is picked by the sonix.stream
   object in front of this one.

 NOTES
   The same number is set on the sonix.stream object as a selector, where it
   defaults to 0 and matches a camera of any vendor. Which camera answered
   is only known once a stream header has been read.

sonix.demuxer/MMM_Pull

 NAME
   MMM_Pull -- Pulls picture data from a sonix.stream source. (V1)

 SYNOPSIS
   ULONG DoMethod(Object *obj, MMM_Pull, ULONG port, APTR buffer, ULONG
   length);

 FUNCTION
   Port 1 hands out the picture of the frame which is in the buffer, and
   waits for the camera when the buffer is empty, which is where a live
   source spends its time. A pull which asks for more than is left of the
   frame returns the rest of it and a short count; the next call reads the
   next frame. The pictures are handed over exactly as they came out of the
   buffer, no scaling and no colour conversion happen here.

   Port 0 is a passthrough to the source and is what the class uses itself
   to read the stream header and the frames. An application asking for it
   gets the raw framed bytes, which is what the recognition code of this
   class does to find the stream header; the class then restores what it
   peeked.

   The class implements no seeking, and it never reports MMERR_END_OF_DATA
   for a live source. It only fails when the camera is unplugged or stops
   answering, which comes back as MMERR_IO_ERROR.

   A stream which has lost its framing stays refused: a NULL buffer, a
   length which is not positive or a port other than 0 or 1 is refused with
   MMERR_WRONG_ARGUMENTS, and a pull on a class which has already given up
   on the stream returns 0 with the error of that first failure.

 INPUTS
   obj - object to perform method on.
   port - number of port, 0 for the raw stream or 1 for the pictures.
   buffer - destination buffer for the data.
   length - amount of data to be pulled in *bytes*.

 RESULT
   Number of bytes pulled, secondary error information via MMA_ErrorCode.

 SEE ALSO
   multimedia.class/MMA_ErrorCode


